+## Shorts cover format and safe area
+
+For every channel, compose portrait Shorts covers so the face, main visual clue, and all essential in-image lettering, including outlines and shadows, remain inside the centered 2:3 crop of the 9:16 master with visible margins. At 1080×1920 this crop is 1080×1620, inset 150 px at top and bottom; scale proportionally. This is a conservative project working guide based on observed cards, not a YouTube specification or a substitute for the actual destination. The area outside the safe crop is not an empty or background-only zone: the environment, scenery and nonessential parts of people and objects may continue naturally into it. Stage the entire 9:16 master as one coherent complete image, with intact anatomy, believable occlusion and no arbitrary horizontal cutoffs; do not hide important faces, text or story clues outside the safe crop. In generation prompts describe the protected content and natural full-frame continuation; do not instruct the generator to leave edge bands empty or background only. Check current official upload guidance and the target surface before uploading. Channel profiles determine visual style, not whether content must remain readable.
+
+Inspect the actual full portrait and centered 2:3 crop at phone-relevant sizes (for example 360×640, 360×540 and 180×270), then inspect the real target card/Studio preview when available, including relevant UI overlays. If the actual crop differs, adapt to the actual surface and recheck. Separately record mobile/device verification and any limitations; a local preview or simulated phone-width layout does not prove physical-device or live YouTube fit. If lettering or the visual clue is clipped, obscured or unreadable, revise the generated cover and repeat the affected checks before acceptance. For other formats and aspect ratios, assess actual crops, overlays and readability for the intended surfaces; do not apply the 2:3 geometry blindly. A YouTube metadata title is separate from lettering embedded in the image.
+
